==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
*{{l|ang|fregnan|freġnan}}, {{l|ang|frinan|frīnan}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|fregnanan|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|preḱ-||to ask|lang=ine-pro}}.

Cognate with Old Saxon ''[[fregnan]]'', Old Norse ''[[fregna]]'', Gothic {{term|𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌷𐌽𐌰𐌽|tr=fraihnan|lang=got}}, and related to Old High German {{term|fraga|frāga|lang=goh}} (German ''[[Frage]]'').  The Indo-European root also gave Old Persian ''aparsam'' ‘I asked’{{rfscript|Cuneiform}}, Irish ''[[arco]]'', Welsh ''[[archaf]]'', Old Armenian {{term|հարց||tr=harcʿ|lang=xcl}}, Tocharian A ''prak-'', Tocharian B ''prek-''{{rfscript|Tocharian}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈfrijnɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|type=strong|class=III|head=friġnan}} (''third-person singular present'' '''[[frigneþ|friġneþ]]''', ''third-person singular preterite'' '''[[frægn|fræġn]]''', ''preterite plural'' '''[[frugnon|fruġnon]]''', ''past participle'' ('''ġe''')'''[[frugnen|fruġnen]]''')

# to [[ask]], [[pray]]

